  "nameStory": "The Sunflower Mining Bee gets its name from its strong preference for sunflower blooms, where it's most commonly spotted gathering nectar and pollen. Like all mining bees, it earns the 'mining' part of its name from its habit of digging tunnels in the ground to create underground nests, much like a tiny miner working below the surface.",
  "description": "The Sunflower Mining Bee is a solitary ground-nesting bee that's roughly the size of a honeybee, measuring about half an inch long. These industrious pollinators have a fuzzy, brownish-black body with lighter bands across their abdomen and pale yellow to white facial markings that help distinguish them from other bees. Unlike social bees that live in colonies, each female works alone to dig her own underground burrow where she'll raise her young. They're excellent pollinators of sunflowers and other late-summer flowers.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Sunflower preference",
      "description": "Most easily found on sunflower blooms from late summer into fall, where they move methodically from flower to flower with a distinctive hovering flight pattern",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Facial markings",
      "description": "Look for pale yellow or whitish markings on the face, especially prominent in males, giving them a masked appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Body texture",
      "description": "Dense, soft fuzzy hair covers the body, feeling almost velvety if you could touch one, with a brownish-black base color",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Ground nests",
      "description": "Small, penny-sized holes in bare or sparsely vegetated soil mark their nest entrances, often with a small pile of excavated dirt nearby",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flight behavior",
      "description": "Flies with a distinctive stop-and-go pattern, hovering briefly before moving to the next flower, less frantic than honeybees",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for Sunflower Mining Bees in open areas with both flowering plants and suitable nesting sites - sandy or well-drained soils in gardens, fields, roadsides, and prairie-like areas. They're often found in residential areas where sunflowers are grown, as well as along farm edges and in community gardens. The females need bare or lightly vegetated ground for digging their burrows, so they favor areas that aren't densely covered with grass or mulch.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Adults are not active yet, but overwintering larvae are developing underground in their mother's carefully provisioned burrows.",
    "summer": "Early to mid-summer sees the emergence of new adults, though peak activity comes later. They begin to appear on early sunflower varieties and other composite flowers.",
    "fall": "Peak season for activity as they're most commonly seen on late-blooming sunflowers, goldenrod, and asters. This is the best time to spot them and observe their nesting behavior.",
    "winter": "No adults are active above ground. Eggs and developing larvae wait out the cold season in underground burrows, protected by the provisions their mothers collected."
  },
  "funFact": "A single female Sunflower Mining Bee can excavate a burrow up to 8 inches deep using only her mandibles and legs, creating multiple side chambers where she'll store perfectly formed balls of pollen mixed with nectar - essentially making underground 'protein bars' for each of her future babies!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most commonly confused with honeybees when seen on flowers, but Sunflower Mining Bees are hairier, have more prominent facial markings, and fly with that characteristic stop-and-hover pattern rather than the steady flight of honeybees. Memory trick: if it looks 'fluffier' and keeps pausing between flowers like it's window shopping, it's likely a mining bee.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Despite being called 'sunflower' mining bees, they'll visit many types of flowers - the name simply reflects where they're most easily found and observed. Don't be surprised to see them on asters, goldenrod, or even garden zinnias, especially later in the season when sunflowers may be finishing up.",
  "compareHints": "Among New England's many Andrena species, the Sunflower Mining Bee is most easily distinguished by its late-season activity period and strong sunflower preference - most other mining bees are spring or early summer specialists on different flowers. The similar Aster Mining Bee overlaps in timing but prefers asters over sunflowers, while spring species like the Cherry Mining Bee are active months earlier on fruit tree blossoms.",
